= Solution
{parent=1}

Expanding the <cross product> gives $\mathbf u\times\mathbf v=(bc,-ac,a)$. Comparing its three components in order from the last to the first gives $a=2$, $ac=6$, and $bc=3$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{a=2,\qquad b=1,\qquad c=3}.
$$
The nonzero value of $a$ permits division to find $c$, and the resulting nonzero $c$ determines $b$.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <chain rule> identifies an <antiderivative> directly:
$$
\frac{d}{dx}e^{\sin(x^2)}=2x\cos(x^2)e^{\sin(x^2)}.
$$
The upper limit in the PDF is $\sqrt{\pi/2}$. By the <fundamental theorem of calculus>, the required <integral> is
$$
\boxed{\frac12\left[e^{\sin(x^2)}\right]_0^{\sqrt{\pi/2}}=\frac{e-1}{2}}.
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The <determinant> is $2\cdot4-3\cdot1=5\ne0$, so the <matrix inverse> exists. Using the two-by-two <adjugate matrix> formula gives
$$
\boxed{A^{-1}=\frac15\begin{pmatrix}4&-1\\-3&2\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
Multiplication on either side gives the <identity matrix>, which also checks the signs of the off-diagonal entries.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Multiply by the <complex conjugate> of the denominator:
$$
\frac{2+3i}{4-5i}=\frac{(2+3i)(4+5i)}{4^2+5^2}
=\frac{-7+22i}{41}.
$$
Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> are respectively
$$
\boxed{-\frac7{41},\qquad\frac{22}{41}}.
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
