= Solution
{parent=5}

Near $x=2$, use $f(x)=2\ln x$, so $f'(2)=1$ and $f''(2)=-1/2$. The <Taylor series> therefore begins
$$
\boxed{\ln(x^2)=\ln4+(x-2)-\frac14(x-2)^2+O((x-2)^3)}.
$$
The constant term is one of the requested three terms. The local expression $2\ln x$ is legitimate because this neighbourhood has $x>0$.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

The <partial derivatives> are $f_{xx}=4f$ and $f_t=\omega f$. Since the <exponential function> never vanishes, substituting into the <partial differential equation> gives $4=6\omega$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\omega=\frac23}.
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

The implication means that $A\subseteq B$, so $A\cap B=A$. By the definition of <conditional probability>,
$$
\boxed{P(A\mid B)=\frac{P(A\cap B)}{P(B)}
=\frac{1/3}{1/2}=\frac23}.
$$
This implication gives $P(B\mid A)=1$; it does not make the two <events> independent.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

In <Cartesian coordinates>, calculate the <divergence> by differentiating each component with respect to its matching coordinate:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=2x+x+6z=3x+6z}.
$$
For the <curl>, take the alternating cross-derivatives:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F
=(\partial_yF_z-\partial_zF_y,\partial_zF_x-\partial_xF_z,
\partial_xF_y-\partial_yF_x)=(0,2x,3y)}.
$$

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

Parametrize the directed segment by $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,0)$, $0\leq t\leq1$. Its tangent is $(1,1,0)$, while the restricted <vector field> is $(t^3,t^3,0)$. The <line integral> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1\mathbf G(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\mathbf r'(t)\,dt
=\int_0^12t^3\,dt=\frac12}.
$$
Alternatively, $\mathbf G=\nabla(x^2y^2/2)$, so the <fundamental theorem for line integrals> gives the same endpoint difference.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

The <derivative> is $f'(x)=2x\cos(x^2-2)$. Thus a <stationary point> occurs either at $x=0$ or where $x^2-2=\pi/2+k\pi$. Since $-2\leq x^2-2<2$ on the specified open interval, the only allowed cosine zeros are $-\pi/2$ and $\pi/2$. The five stationary abscissae are
$$
\boxed{x=0,\qquad x=\pm\sqrt{2-\frac\pi2},\qquad
x=\pm\sqrt{2+\frac\pi2}}.
$$
Their ordinates are $-\sin2$, $-1$, and $1$, respectively. For completeness, $f''=2\cos(x^2-2)-4x^2\sin(x^2-2)$ shows that the inner pair are <local minima> and the outer pair are <local maxima>; $x=0$ is also a <local maximum> because $2\cos2<0$.

= 11
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Solving $(A-4I)\mathbf v=0$ gives $v_2=0$ and $v_1=v_3$. A convenient normalized <eigenvector> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf e_1=\frac1{\sqrt2}(1,0,1),\qquad A\mathbf e_1=4\mathbf e_1}.
$$
The displayed nonzero <vector> proves that four is an <eigenvalue>. Every nonzero multiple represents the same eigendirection.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <vector> perpendicular to $\mathbf e_1$ has the form $\mathbf v=(s,t,-s)$. Direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
A\mathbf v=(-2s,-2t,2s)=-2\mathbf v.
$$
Thus every **nonzero** such <vector> is an <eigenvector> with $\boxed{\lambda=-2}$. The zero <vector> must be excluded from the literal claim about “any” perpendicular <vector>, since an <eigenvector> is nonzero. The <eigenspace> is two-dimensional. Equivalently, $A=-2I+6\mathbf e_1\mathbf e_1^{\mathsf T}$ exhibits the <spectrum of a scalar matrix plus a rank-one projector>.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Choose two perpendicular unit directions in the $-2$ <eigenspace>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf e_1=\frac{(1,0,1)}{\sqrt2},\qquad
\mathbf e_2=\frac{(1,0,-1)}{\sqrt2},\qquad
\mathbf e_3=(0,1,0)}.
$$
Their pairwise <dot products> vanish and their <Euclidean norms> are one. They form an <orthonormal basis> of <eigenvectors>, with <eigenvalues> $4,-2,-2$, as guaranteed for a real <symmetric matrix> by the <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices>.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <trace> is the sum of the <eigenvalues>, counting multiplicity, while the <determinant> is their product:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{tr}A=1-2+1=0=4-2-2,\qquad
\det A=16=4(-2)(-2)}.
$$
A direct expansion along the middle row checks the latter: $(-2)(1-9)=16$.

= e
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Using the ordered <orthonormal basis> chosen in (c), the column <matrix> is
$$
\boxed{B=\begin{pmatrix}
1/\sqrt2&1/\sqrt2&0\\
0&0&1\\
1/\sqrt2&-1/\sqrt2&0
\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
Other signs or another <orthonormal> choice within the repeated <eigenspace> are equally valid, provided the same choice is retained in the remaining parts.

= f
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The $(i,j)$ entry of $B^{\mathsf T}B$ is $\mathbf e_i\cdot\mathbf e_j=\delta_{ij}$. Hence $B$ is an <orthogonal matrix>, whose <matrix inverse> equals its <transpose>:
$$
\boxed{B^{-1}=B^{\mathsf T}=\begin{pmatrix}
1/\sqrt2&0&1/\sqrt2\\
1/\sqrt2&0&-1/\sqrt2\\
0&1&0
\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
This obtains the inverse without unnecessary cofactor calculations.

= g
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

The <eigenvector> equations give $AB=B\operatorname{diag}(4,-2,-2)$. Multiplying on the left by $B^{-1}$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{B^{-1}AB=\begin{pmatrix}4&0&0\\0&-2&0\\0&0&-2\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
This is an <orthogonal diagonalization>: the <linear map> acts independently by multiplication by its <eigenvalues> in the new <orthonormal basis>. The repeated <eigenvalue> explains the freedom to rotate the last two basis directions.

