= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Apply the <fundamental theorem of calculus> separately to the upper and lower limits. No evaluation of the <integral> is needed:
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial I_{n,k}}{\partial x}=\cos^kx(1-\sin^3x)^n,\qquad
\frac{\partial I_{n,k}}{\partial y}=-\cos^ky(1-\sin^3y)^n}.
$$
The first result has no $y$ dependence and the second no $x$ dependence, so the mixed <partial derivative> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial^2I_{n,k}}{\partial x\partial y}=0}.
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Set $s=\sin u$, so $ds=\cos u\,du$, and write $J_n=I_{n,1}(\pi/2,0)$. The <integration by substitution> gives $J_n=\int_0^1(1-s^3)^n\,ds$. For $n>0$, <integration by parts> yields
$$
J_n=[s(1-s^3)^n]_0^1+3n\int_0^1s^3(1-s^3)^{n-1}\,ds.
$$
Both endpoint terms vanish. Use $s^3=1-(1-s^3)$ inside the remaining <integral>:
$$
J_n=3n(J_{n-1}-J_n).
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{I_{n,1}(\pi/2,0)=\frac{3n}{3n+1}I_{n-1,1}(\pi/2,0),\qquad
f(n)=\frac{3n}{3n+1}}.
$$
This is the $p=3$ instance of the <power-complement integral recurrence>, with initial value $J_0=1$.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply the <power-complement integral recurrence> three times, starting from $I_{0,1}(\pi/2,0)=1$:
$$
\boxed{I_{3,1}(\pi/2,0)=\frac9{10}\frac67\frac34=\frac{81}{140}}.
$$
Each factor corresponds to reducing $n$ by one; the final unreduced <integral> is just $\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos u\,du=1$.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Subtract the integrands first, using $\cos u-\cos^3u=\cos u\sin^2u$. Then set $s=\sin u$:
$$
I_{n,1}(\pi/2,0)-I_{n,3}(\pi/2,0)
=\int_0^1s^2(1-s^3)^n\,ds.
$$
The further <integration by substitution> $w=s^3$ makes $dw=3s^2ds$, so
$$
\boxed{I_{n,1}(\pi/2,0)-I_{n,3}(\pi/2,0)
=\frac13\int_0^1(1-w)^n\,dw=\frac1{3(n+1)}}.
$$
This also holds at $n=0$.

= 13
{parent=Paper 2}
