= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <characteristic equation> is $r^2+r-2=(r-1)(r+2)=0$, giving $y=Ae^x+Be^{-2x}$. The <initial conditions> give $A+B=1$ and $A-2B=-1$. Solving yields $A=1/3$, $B=2/3$, hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac13e^x+\frac23e^{-2x}}.
$$
The two distinct characteristic roots supply all solutions of this second-order <linear ordinary differential equation>.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <characteristic equation> has the repeated root $r=-3$. Its <homogeneous solution> is therefore $(C_1+C_2x)e^{-3x}$. For a <particular solution>, try $y_p=Ax+B$. Substitution gives $9Ax+6A+9B=3x+1$, so $A=1/3$ and $B=-1/9$. Thus the <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=(C_1+C_2x)e^{-3x}+\frac x3-\frac19}.
$$
The extra factor $x$ in the second homogeneous term is required by the repeated root.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <characteristic equation> factors as $(r+1)(r+2)=0$, giving $y_h=C_1e^{-x}+C_2e^{-2x}$. The <superposition principle> lets us find <particular solutions> for the two forcing terms separately. A trial $Ax+B$ gives $2A=1$ and $3A+2B=0$, so it is $x/2-3/4$. A trial $Ce^{3x}$ gives $(9+9+2)C=1$, so $C=1/20$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=C_1e^{-x}+C_2e^{-2x}+\frac x2-\frac34+\frac1{20}e^{3x}}.
$$

