= Solution
{parent=i}

Use outward <normal vectors> to the two faces. The <cross products>
$$
\mathbf n_1=\overrightarrow{OP}\times\overrightarrow{OR}=(1,-2,2),\qquad
\mathbf n_2=\overrightarrow{OR}\times\overrightarrow{OQ}=(-2,1,-1)
$$
point away from the opposite vertices, since $\mathbf n_1\cdot\overrightarrow{OQ}=-3$ and $\mathbf n_2\cdot\overrightarrow{OP}=-3$. Their <Euclidean norms> are $3$ and $\sqrt6$, and their <dot product> is $-6$. The <interior dihedral angle of a convex polyhedron> is supplementary to the outward-normal angle, so
$$
\boxed{\cos\theta=-\frac{\mathbf n_1\cdot\mathbf n_2}{|\mathbf n_1||\mathbf n_2|}
=\frac2{\sqrt6}=\sqrt{\frac23}}.
$$
This also equals the cosine of the acute angle between the unoriented face planes. The outward-normal angle itself has cosine $-\sqrt{2/3}$; keeping that distinction avoids a sign ambiguity.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the triangular face, take the <cross product> of two edges and divide by two:
$$
\frac12\overrightarrow{PQ}\times\overrightarrow{PR}
=\frac12(-1,1,0)\times(-2,0,1)=\frac12(1,1,2).
$$
The origin lies on the opposite side from this normal, since $(1,1,2)\cdot\overrightarrow{PO}=-3$. Thus this is already the outward <vector area>, with
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{\sqrt6}{2},\qquad\hat{\mathbf n}=\frac{(1,1,2)}{\sqrt6},\qquad
A\hat{\mathbf n}=\left(\frac12,\frac12,1\right)}.
$$
The <vector areas of a closed polyhedron sum to zero>, so the sum for the remaining three faces is
$$
\boxed{\left(-\frac12,-\frac12,-1\right)}.
$$
\Image[paper-2-tetrahedron.png]
{title=Tetrahedron with its highlighted face PQR and outward unit normal}

Their individual outward <vector areas> are $(0,0,-3/2)$, $(1/2,-1,1)$, and $(-1,1/2,-1/2)$, which directly check that sum.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The normal from (ii) gives the <plane> equation $x+y+2z=3$. The <distance from a point to a plane> is the absolute value of the normalized plane equation at that point. At the origin this gives
$$
\boxed{d=\frac3{\sqrt{1^2+1^2+2^2}}=\frac3{\sqrt6}=\frac{\sqrt6}{2}}.
$$
For a geometric check, the perpendicular foot is $H=(1/2,1/2,1)$, parallel to the normal and satisfying the plane equation. Its <Euclidean norm> is the same distance.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <volume> of a <parallelepiped> is the absolute <scalar triple product> of its three edge <vectors>. Since $\overrightarrow{OP}\times\overrightarrow{OQ}=(0,0,3)$,
$$
\boxed{\mathcal V=\left|\bigl(\overrightarrow{OP}\times\overrightarrow{OQ}\bigr)
\cdot\overrightarrow{OR}\right|=3}.
$$
The factor $1/6$ appropriate to a <tetrahedron> must not be applied to this parallelepiped.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Inside the <sphere>, the <electric field> has the form $\mathbf E=\nabla[-kr^2/(2a^3)]$, including at the origin. Thus it is a <conservative vector field>. Outside, use the <gradient of a radial function> to obtain
$$
\mathbf E=k\nabla\frac1r=-\frac{k\mathbf r}{r^3}.
$$
This is also an explicit <potential of a conservative vector field>, single-valued throughout the exterior region. Equivalently, the <curl> vanishes in both regions: for a radial coefficient $h(r)$, $\nabla\times(h\mathbf r)=\nabla h\times\mathbf r+h\nabla\times\mathbf r=0$ because $\nabla h$ is radial. The interior formula avoids any apparent $1/r$ issue at the origin. The vector-field potentials here have the opposite sign from the <electric potential> convention used in (b).

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a radial <electric potential>, $\mathbf E=-V'(r)\mathbf r/r$. Comparing radial coefficients gives
$$
V'_{\rm in}(r)=\frac{kr}{a^3},\qquad V'_{\rm out}(r)=\frac{k}{r^2}.
$$
Integrating these one-variable <derivatives> gives the general local expressions
$$
\boxed{V_{\rm in}(r)=\frac{kr^2}{2a^3}+C_{\rm in},\qquad
V_{\rm out}(r)=-\frac kr+C_{\rm out}}.
$$
The two constants are initially independent because these integrations were performed in separate radial regions.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The condition at infinity gives $C_{\rm out}=0$. Matching the <electric potential> at $r=a$ gives
$$
\frac{k}{2a}+C_{\rm in}=-\frac ka,
\qquad C_{\rm in}=-\frac{3k}{2a}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{V(r)=\begin{cases}
\dfrac{k}{2a^3}(r^2-3a^2),&0\leq r<a,\\
-\dfrac kr,&r\geq a.
\end{cases}}
$$
This <function> is finite and differentiable at the origin. At the joining radius, both $V$ and $V'$ match, giving $V(a)=-k/a$ and $V'(a)=k/a^2$. Thus the <electric field> is continuous there as well as being conservative on each side. This is the <potential of a uniform spherical source>, with source coefficient $3k/a^3$.

\Image[paper-2-radial-potential.png]
{title=Continuous radial potential and inward electric field, normalized at the sphere boundary}

