= Solution
{parent=a}

The <multivariable chain rule> gives
$$
\frac{dF}{dt}=(3x^2yz+y)(-3\sin t)+(x^3z+x)(3\cos t)+(x^3y+1)2.
$$
Substituting the three parametrizing functions also writes the complete <derivative> as
$$
\frac{dF}{dt}=162\cos^3t\sin t
+162t(\cos^4t-3\cos^2t\sin^2t)+9\cos2t+2.
$$
At $t=\pi/2$, the point is $(0,3,\pi)$, its velocity is $(-3,0,2)$, and $\nabla F=(3,0,1)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\left.\frac{dF}{dt}\right|_{t=\pi/2}=\nabla F\cdot\mathbf r'=-9+2=-7}.
$$

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write the <differential one-form> as $M\,dx+N\,dy$. An <exact differential form> must satisfy equality of mixed <partial derivatives>, $M_y=N_x$. Here
$$
M_y=x-2y,\qquad N_x=x-ay.
$$
Equality at every $(x,y)$ holds precisely when $\boxed{a=2}$. Since these <polynomials> are smooth on the simply connected <plane>, the closed-form condition is also sufficient for exactness; the explicit <potential of a conservative vector field> constructed in (c) verifies that sufficiency directly.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

With $a=2$, integrate the $dx$ coefficient with respect to $x$:
$$
\Phi(x,y)=\frac{x^3}{3}+\frac{x^2y}{2}-xy^2+g(y).
$$
Its other <partial derivative> is $\Phi_y=x^2/2-2xy+g'(y)$. Matching the $dy$ coefficient gives $g'=0$. Therefore the <differential equation> is $d\Phi=0$, and its implicit <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{x^3}{3}+\frac{x^2y}{2}-xy^2=C}.
$$
These are <level curves> of the <potential of a conservative vector field>. Where $N\ne0$, they give the usual graph solution $dy/dx=-M/N$. At $N=0$, the implicit description retains possible vertical tangents instead of dividing by zero.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A cubic version of <Taylor's theorem> requires the corresponding higher differentiability, rather than only one <derivative>. For example, if $f$ has four continuous <derivatives> on the interval between $a$ and $x$, then
$$
f(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2
+\frac{f^{(3)}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+R_3(x),
$$
where the Lagrange form of the <Taylor remainder> is
$$
\boxed{R_3(x)=\frac{f^{(4)}(\xi)}{4!}(x-a)^4}
$$
for some $\xi$ strictly between $a$ and $x$ when they differ. At $x=a$, the remainder is zero. These hypotheses apply to both functions used below.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The required <derivatives> are $f'(x)=1/(2\sqrt x)$, $f''(x)=-1/(4x^{3/2})$, $f^{(3)}(x)=3/(8x^{5/2})$, and $f^{(4)}(x)=-15/(16x^{7/2})$. The four-term <Taylor polynomial> about one, evaluated at two, is
$$
\boxed{\sqrt2\approx1+\frac12-\frac18+\frac1{16}=\frac{23}{16}}.
$$
The <Taylor remainder> is $R_3(2)=-5/(128\xi^{7/2})$ for some $1<\xi<2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\left|\sqrt2-\frac{23}{16}\right|\leq\frac5{128}}.
$$
Its negative sign also shows that this approximation is an overestimate. Bounding the fourth <derivative> throughout the interval establishes the requested error estimate without using a decimal value of $\sqrt2$.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Factor $1+e^x=2e^{x/2}\cosh(x/2)$. This gives
$$
\ln(1+e^x)=\ln2+\frac x2+\ln\cosh\frac x2.
$$
The last <function> is even. Its <Taylor series> begins $\ln\cosh(x/2)=x^2/8+O(x^4)$, since $\cosh(x/2)=1+x^2/8+O(x^4)$ and $\ln(1+w)=w+O(w^2)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\ln(1+e^x)=\ln2+\frac x2+\frac{x^2}{8}+0x^3+O(x^4)}.
$$
In particular, the cubic coefficient is zero; the identity explains its cancellation before any lengthy differentiation.

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

There are eighteen equally likely first choices. Counting the relevant subsets gives
$$
\boxed{P(A)=\frac{13}{18},\qquad P(B)=\frac{10}{18}=\frac59,\qquad
P(A\cap B)=\frac8{18}=\frac49}.
$$
For <conditional probabilities>, restrict the denominator to the conditioning subset:
$$
\boxed{P(A\mid B)=\frac8{10}=\frac45,\qquad P(B\mid A)=\frac8{13}}.
$$
The five counts come from the same finite <sample space>, so no independence assumption is involved.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Insert the values from (a) into <Bayes' theorem>:
$$
\frac{P(A\mid B)P(B)}{P(A)}
=\frac{(4/5)(5/9)}{13/18}=\frac8{13}=P(B\mid A).
$$
Thus the identity is **satisfied**. Equivalently, both $P(A\mid B)P(B)$ and $P(B\mid A)P(A)$ equal the joint <probability> $4/9$.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Condition on whether the first selection was a red apple. Its <probability> is $3/18$. If it was, two red apples remain among seventeen fruits; otherwise three remain. The <law of total probability> gives
$$
\boxed{P(\text{second is red apple})
=\frac3{18}\frac2{17}+\frac{15}{18}\frac3{17}=\frac16}.
$$
The same result follows from <exchangeability>: before either selection is observed, each ordered draw position has the original red-apple fraction $3/18$, despite dependence between the draws.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $C$ denote a green pear on the first draw and $D$ a red apple on the second. The two categories are disjoint, so after $C$ there are still three red apples among seventeen remaining fruits. Thus $P(C\cap D)=(8/18)(3/17)$. Using $P(D)=1/6$ from (c), <conditional probability> gives
$$
\boxed{P(C\mid D)=\frac{(8/18)(3/17)}{1/6}=\frac8{17}}.
$$
Equivalently, conditioning on the second fruit being one particular category removes a red apple and leaves the other seventeen fruits symmetrically eligible for the first position.

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
