= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Leibniz integral rule> accounts for both the moving upper endpoint and the integrand's dependence on $a$:
$$
\boxed{I'(a)=f(a,a)+\int_0^a\frac{\partial f}{\partial a}(x,a)\,dx}.
$$
For the specified integrand this gives $I'(a)=e^{-a^2}-\int_0^ax e^{-ax}\,dx$. When $a\ne0$, <integration by parts> gives
$$
\int_0^ax e^{-ax}\,dx=-e^{-a^2}+\frac{1-e^{-a^2}}{a^2},
$$
so
$$
\boxed{I'(a)=2e^{-a^2}-\frac{1-e^{-a^2}}{a^2}\quad(a\ne0)}.
$$
For the requested independent check, direct integration gives $I(a)=(1-e^{-a^2})/a$ at $a\ne0$. Its <derivative> is $[2a^2e^{-a^2}-(1-e^{-a^2})]/a^2$, agreeing with the result above. At zero the apparent singularity is removable: $I(a)=a-a^3/2+O(a^5)$, so $\boxed{I(0)=0,\ I'(0)=1}$.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $0<x<1$,
$$
\frac{x^b-1}{\ln x}=\int_0^b x^s\,ds.
$$
In particular its <absolute value> is bounded by $|b|x^{\min(b,0)}$, proving convergence when $b>-1$; the apparent endpoint singularity at one is removable, with value $b$. On any compact parameter interval with lower bound $b_0>-1$, the differentiated integrand is $x^b$, dominated by $x^{b_0}$. The <dominated convergence theorem> therefore justifies <differentiation under the integral sign>:
$$
\boxed{J'(b)=\int_0^1x^b\,dx=\frac1{b+1}}.
$$
Hence $J(b)=\ln(b+1)+C$. Since the integrand is identically zero at $b=0$, $J(0)=0$ fixes $C=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{J(b)=\ln(b+1),\qquad b>-1}.
$$
This proves the <logarithmic integral representation of the natural logarithm>, including its parameter range.

