= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write the kernel as $K(s,t)=(4\pi t)^{-1/2}e^{-s^2/(4t)}$, with $t>0$. Its <partial derivatives> are
$$
K_t=\left(-\frac1{2t}+\frac{s^2}{4t^2}\right)K,
\qquad K_{ss}=\left(-\frac1{2t}+\frac{s^2}{4t^2}\right)K.
$$
Thus the <heat kernel> itself satisfies $K_t=K_{ss}$. The proposed <convolution> consequently gives
$$
u_t(x,t)=\int_{\mathbb R}\varphi(\xi)K_t(x-\xi,t)\,d\xi
=\int_{\mathbb R}\varphi(\xi)K_{ss}(x-\xi,t)\,d\xi
=u_{xx}(x,t).
$$
This verifies the <heat equation>. The parameter is positive time; the real integral formula is not defined at negative time.

The passage of <derivatives> through the <integral> needs justification. A sufficient condition is the Gaussian-weighted absolute integrability in <heat-kernel convergence for Gaussian-integrable initial data>; it holds, in particular, for data of <polynomial growth>. On compact positive-time intervals strictly below an integrable later time, the differentiated kernels have an integrable Gaussian bound, so <differentiation under the integral sign> applies.

Even if the PDF's assumed convergence is read as ordinary conditional convergence, one can justify the same calculation. Choose a later time $T>t$ and let $H(\xi)$ be a primitive of $\varphi(\xi)e^{-\xi^2/(4T)}$. Convergence at spatial point zero and time $T$ makes $H$ bounded on both tails. Put $q(x,\xi,t)=K(x-\xi,t)e^{\xi^2/(4T)}$. This <function>, its spatial and time <derivatives>, and their $\xi$-derivatives decay as Gaussians when $t<T$. <Integration by parts> rewrites the <convolution> as $-\int Hq_\xi\,d\xi$, with vanishing endpoint terms. Boundedness of $H$ now permits differentiation under this absolutely convergent <integral>; since $q_t=q_{xx}$, the same equation follows. Thus one need not silently equate conditional and absolute convergence.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The indicated <integration by substitution> gives
$$
u(x,t)=\frac1{\sqrt\pi}\int_{\mathbb R}
\varphi(x+2\sqrt t\,\eta)e^{-\eta^2}\,d\eta.
$$
Pointwise, the argument of $\varphi$ tends to $x$. Since the <Gaussian integral> is $\int e^{-\eta^2}d\eta=\sqrt\pi$, the expected limit is $\varphi(x)$. To justify it for unbounded data, use the <approximate identity> property rather than assuming a uniform bound on $\varphi$.

In the original variable, normalization gives
$$
u(x,t)-\varphi(x)=\int_{\mathbb R}K(x-\xi,t)\bigl(\varphi(\xi)-\varphi(x)\bigr)\,d\xi.
$$
For $|\xi-x|<\delta$, <continuity> makes the difference smaller than any prescribed $\varepsilon$ if $\delta$ is small enough, and the kernel mass is at most one. On the complement the Gaussian mass tends to zero; the data-weighted tail tends to zero by <heat-kernel convergence for Gaussian-integrable initial data>. This proves
$$
\boxed{\lim_{t\downarrow0}u(x,t)=\varphi(x)}.
$$
For <heat-kernel convergence from a conditionally convergent Gaussian integral>, the same tail conclusion follows using the bounded primitive of $(\varphi(\xi)-\varphi(x))e^{-\xi^2/(4T)}$. The factor $q=K(x-\xi,t)e^{\xi^2/(4T)}$ has its maximum at $\xi=xT/(T-t)$, approaching $x$. For sufficiently small $t$, it is monotone on each of the two tails outside $|\xi-x|<\delta$, and its values at the tail endpoints tend to zero exponentially. <Integration by parts> bounds each tail by a constant times its endpoint value, proving the same limit without an absolute-convergence assumption. The approach $t\downarrow0$ is from positive time.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Insert the linear initial profile into the transformed <integral> from (b):
$$
u(x,t)=\frac a{\sqrt\pi}\int_{\mathbb R}
(x+2\sqrt t\,\eta)e^{-\eta^2}\,d\eta.
$$
The constant part integrates to $ax$. The other part is an absolutely integrable <odd function>, so its <integral> vanishes. Hence
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=ax\qquad(t\geq0)}.
$$
As a direct check, $u_t=u_{xx}=0$ and $u(x,0)=ax$. Thus a linear <temperature> profile is stationary on the whole line. More generally, every <affine function> is preserved by this <heat-kernel solution>.
